## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to model selections of multiple disjoint subarrays with an exact count — a common dynamic programming pattern in interviews.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefix**: State dp[i][t] tracks the best total using the first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) calculation of any subarray sum
- **Backpointers**: Store decisions to reconstruct the chosen subarrays
- **Edge cases**: Handle when n < k or when all values are negative

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skip: true };
      }
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (t > 0 && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t - 1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const p = prev[i][t];
    if (p.skip) { i--; continue; }
    result.push([p.l, i]);
    i = p.l - 1; t = p.t;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: result.reverse() };
}
```

